<div class="section active" id="tab-notes">

  <div class="chapter-title">Chapter 1: Introduction to Principles of Accounting</div>
  <div class="chapter-sub">Complete notes covering all definitions, concepts, and key points from your textbook.</div>

  <!-- 1. Definition of Accounting -->
  <div class="topic open">
    <div class="topic-header" onclick="toggleTopic(this)">
      <span class="topic-title">1. Definition of Accounting</span>
      <span class="topic-arrow">▾</span>
    </div>
    <div class="topic-body">
      <div class="def-body">Accounting deals with communication of business activities of a firm to the individual and various sections within the firm and also to outsiders. It is an art of recording all business transactions and events of financial character in a prescribed manner, classifying them in a significant manner, analyzing classified records to prepare summary results communicated to concerned parties in shape of reports and statements.</div>
      <div class="section-label">Key Definitions by Authorities</div>
      <ul class="bullet-list">
        <li><strong>Dupree & Marder:</strong> "The act of collecting, processing, analyzing, interpreting and projecting of financial statements."</li>
        <li><strong>MacFarlan, Ayares & Stove:</strong> "The art of systematically recording, presenting and interpreting the financial information of an enterprise."</li>
        <li><strong>American Accounting Association:</strong> "Accounting is the process of identifying, measuring and communicating economic information to permit informed judgment and decision by user of the information."</li>
        <li><strong>Committee of Institute of Public Accountants:</strong> "The art of recording, classifying and summarizing in a significant manner and in terms of money, transactions and events, which are in part, at least of financial character, interpreting the results thereof."</li>
        <li><strong>Comprehensive Definition:</strong> "The art of recording, classifying and summarizing the business transactions and finally to prepare the financial statements, i.e. income statement and balance sheet."</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="highlight-box">
        <strong>From all definitions, accounting is mainly concerned with:</strong><br>
        1. Recording of business data &nbsp;|&nbsp; 2. Classification of recorded data &nbsp;|&nbsp; 3. Analysis of summarized data &nbsp;|&nbsp; 4. Preparation of statements and reports
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 2. Book Keeping -->
  <div class="topic">
    <div class="topic-header" onclick="toggleTopic(this)">
      <span class="topic-title">2. Book Keeping — Definition & Differences</span>
      <span class="topic-arrow">▾</span>
    </div>
    <div class="topic-body">
      <div class="def-body">"Book" means the "books of accounts" and "keeping" means "maintaining them in proper form and order." Book-keeping is mainly concerned with the recording of business data in proper books.</div>
      <ul class="bullet-list">
        <li>"Book-keeping is an art of recording in books of accounts, transactions in money or money's terms."</li>
        <li>"The systematic recording of financial transaction of an enterprise is known as book-keeping."</li>
        <li>"Book-keeping may be defined as an art of recording business transactions in terms of money under appropriate heads."</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="highlight-box"><strong>Key Fact:</strong> Accounting is much wider than book-keeping. Book-keeping and accounting do NOT have the same meaning.</div>
      <table class="compare-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Points of Difference</th><th>Book-Keeping</th><th>Accounting</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>1. Scope of Work</strong></td><td>First phase in Accounting. Involves recording business transactions in prescribed manner.</td><td>Next step. Includes classification, summarizing data, preparation of statements and ascertaining results.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>2. Job Description</strong></td><td>Job of Book Keeper is of clerical nature.</td><td>Job of Accountant is of technical nature.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>3. Knowledge & Skill</strong></td><td>No professional knowledge required. Sufficient that he can write in prescribed columns.</td><td>Professional knowledge and skill is required for Accountants.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>4. Decisions</strong></td><td>Record maintained by Book Keeper serves as a base for the Accounting process.</td><td>Accounting record is used to make decisions regarding business activities.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 3. Special Fields -->
  <div class="topic">
    <div class="topic-header" onclick="toggleTopic(this)">
      <span class="topic-title">3. Special Fields / Branches of Accounting</span>
      <span class="topic-arrow">▾</span>
    </div>
    <div class="topic-body">
      <ul class="bullet-list">
        <li><strong>Financial Accounting:</strong> Concerns with general accounting system. Records business transactions in books and prepares periodical reports for managers and general public. Prepares journal, ledger, trial balance, income statement and balance sheet.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost Accounting:</strong> Main objective is to control cost of production and distribution. Checks efficiency of producing, selling and administrative departments by applying actual and pre-determined cost techniques. Also shows effects on cost with changes in volume of production and sales.</li>
        <li><strong>Managerial Accounting:</strong> Mainly concerns with selection of best among various alternatives. Uses techniques of historical, estimated and actual data as a device toward positive change.</li>
        <li><strong>Government Accounting:</strong> Method of recording financial transactions of central and provincial governments. Keeps record of expenditure, taxes, revenue and budget of various government departments. Also known as <em>Applied Accounting</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Auditing:</strong> Examination of the accounting record. Purpose is to check finances and accuracy of accounting record and its reconciliation with prescribed rules, policies and procedure.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

